            "Injury kills more Ghanaians between the ages of five and forty-five than any disease, and the pattern is familiar to every casualty nurse: <strong>road traffic crashes</strong> — motorcycle riders and their pillion passengers without helmets, overloaded tro-tros and trucks on the highways at night, pedestrians at the roadside — <strong>falls</strong> from heights and trees, <strong>assaults</strong> with machetes, bottles and fists, <strong>gunshot</strong> and stab wounds in robberies, <strong>farm and industrial</strong> injuries, drowning, and burns. Trauma deaths come in three waves: at the scene from massive brain, heart or great-vessel injury, which only prevention stops; in the first hours from <strong>airway obstruction, chest injury and bleeding</strong>, which is where organised care saves lives; and days later from sepsis and organ failure. The first wave of care is the bystander's, and the nurse who comes upon a crash follows <strong>DRSABC</strong>: <strong>danger</strong> — is the scene safe for me? (traffic, fire, fuel, electricity, a hostile crowd; never become the second casualty); <strong>response</strong> — shout and gently shake the shoulders; <strong>shout for help</strong> and send a named person to call <strong>112</strong> for the National Ambulance Service with the location and the number injured; <strong>airway</strong> opened with a <strong>jaw thrust</strong> rather than a head tilt because the neck may be broken; <strong>breathing</strong> — look, listen and feel for up to ten seconds; and <strong>circulation</strong> — chest compressions if there is no breathing, and otherwise firm <strong>direct pressure</strong> on any bleeding. The casualty is not moved unless there is danger, the head is held still in line with the body, nothing is given by mouth, and she is covered and kept warm. When there are many casualties the nurse triages with the simple sieve: those who can <strong>walk</strong> are green; of the rest, anyone <strong>not breathing after the airway is opened</strong> is dead; a <strong>respiratory rate over 30</strong>, a <strong>capillary refill over two seconds or no radial pulse</strong>, or an inability to <strong>obey commands</strong> makes a red priority; everyone else is yellow.",
