            "The <strong>primary survey</strong> finds and fixes the injuries that kill in the order in which they kill — each problem corrected before the next is looked for, and the whole sequence <strong>restarted from the beginning</strong> whenever the patient deteriorates. It begins, in modern practice, with <strong>catastrophic external haemorrhage</strong>: an artery pumping from a limb is stopped before anything else, by firm direct pressure, packing of the wound, and a <strong>tourniquet</strong> above a limb wound that pressure cannot control, tightened until the bleeding stops and the <strong>time written on it</strong> and on the patient. <strong>A — Airway with cervical spine control</strong>: a patient who answers a question in a clear voice has an open airway and a perfused brain; one who gurgles, snores, has stridor or cannot speak does not, and the mouth is cleared with suction, the jaw thrust forward, an oropharyngeal airway placed in the unconscious patient (a nasopharyngeal airway is avoided when the base of the skull may be fractured — blood or clear fluid from the nose or ear, bruising behind the ear or around both eyes), and the anaesthetist called to intubate a patient with a <strong>Glasgow Coma Scale of 8 or less</strong>, a burned airway or an expanding neck haematoma; throughout, the <strong>neck is held still</strong> by the hands of a team member or by a collar, blocks and tape, because every patient with a significant mechanism, a head injury, neck pain or an altered conscious level has a broken neck until an X-ray or scan says otherwise. <strong>B — Breathing</strong>: high-flow <strong>oxygen at 15 L/min through a non-rebreathing mask</strong> goes on now, not after the X-ray; the nurse counts the rate, reads the saturation, strips the chest and <strong>looks</strong> for wounds, bruising, asymmetry and paradoxical movement, <strong>listens</strong> for air entry on both sides, <strong>feels</strong> for the trachea in the midline, crepitus and tenderness, and looks at the neck veins — because six chest injuries kill within minutes and all are found with the hands and a stethoscope: <strong>tension pneumothorax</strong> (worsening breathlessness, absent breath sounds and a hyper-resonant hemithorax, distended neck veins, the trachea pushed away, collapse — decompressed <strong>at once with a wide-bore cannula</strong> in the fifth intercostal space in the anterior axillary line or the second space in the mid-clavicular line, then a chest drain; never wait for an X-ray), <strong>open pneumothorax</strong> (a sucking chest wound — covered with a dressing <strong>taped on three sides</strong> so that air escapes but does not enter, then a drain), <strong>massive haemothorax</strong> (dull, silent, shocked — a large chest drain, and blood replaced), <strong>flail chest</strong> (a segment moving inwards on inspiration — oxygen, analgesia, and ventilation if it fails), <strong>cardiac tamponade</strong> (shock with distended neck veins and muffled heart sounds after a penetrating wound to the chest — needle or surgical drainage) and airway rupture.",
