          alt: "Head injury: the number matters less than the direction. A score of 12 that was 15 an hour ago is an emergency; a score of 12 that has been 12 all day is not the same thing. Eye opening out of 4: spontaneously 4, to speech 3, to pain 2, no response 1; swollen eyes recorded as not testable. Verbal out of 5: oriented 5, confused conversation 4, words not sentences 3, sounds only 2, none 1; ask in a language she speaks. Motor out of 6: obeys 6, localises 5, withdraws 4, abnormal flexion 3, extension 2, none 1; the best response on the better side. Lowest possible is 3, never 0; 8 or less means the airway is not safe; always record the three parts, not only the sum. Signs of rising pressure: falling conscious level, one pupil enlarging and sluggish, rising pressure with a slowing pulse, vomiting, worsening headache. The lucid interval: arterial bleeding between skull and brain accumulates over hours; the patient wakes, talks normally, then deteriorates. Never discharge a head injury on how well she looks now.",
