            "<strong>Acute pancreatitis</strong> — from gallstones, alcohol, and sometimes drugs, trauma, high triglycerides or the mumps virus — gives severe, constant <strong>epigastric pain boring through to the back</strong>, eased a little by sitting forward and worsened by lying flat, with repeated vomiting, a rapid pulse, fever, a tender distended upper abdomen and, days later in the severe case, bruising of the flanks or around the umbilicus; a <strong>serum amylase or lipase</strong> several times the upper limit confirms it. There is no operation for the inflammation itself, and the nursing is intensive support: <strong>oxygen</strong>, <strong>aggressive intravenous fluid</strong> because litres leak into the retroperitoneum, a catheter and hourly urine output, <strong>opioid analgesia</strong> without apology, a nasogastric tube if she vomits, nothing by mouth at first but <strong>early feeding</strong> as soon as the pain allows because starvation worsens the outcome, four-hourly observations and <strong>blood glucose</strong> (the islets are inflamed), monitoring of <strong>calcium</strong> (it falls and causes tetany), the oxygen saturation (respiratory distress is the commonest cause of death in the first week), the kidney, and the signs of a pseudocyst, necrosis or abscess in the following weeks; antibiotics are not given routinely, alcohol is addressed once she is well, and gallstones are removed before she goes home or soon after. <strong>Acute cholecystitis</strong> gives right upper quadrant pain after a fatty meal <strong>radiating to the right shoulder blade</strong>, with fever, vomiting, tenderness that catches the breath when the examiner's hand presses under the ribs on inspiration (Murphy's sign) and sometimes a palpable gallbladder; it is treated with nil by mouth, fluids, analgesia, antibiotics and cholecystectomy early or after the inflammation settles (lesson 6.1). <strong>Jaundice</strong> with the pain means a stone in the common bile duct, and <strong>fever, jaundice and right upper quadrant pain together</strong> (Charcot's triad) mean <strong>ascending cholangitis</strong> — pus under pressure in the biliary tree, a septic emergency needing antibiotics within the hour and urgent drainage.",
            "<strong>Every woman of reproductive age with abdominal pain is pregnant until the test says otherwise</strong>, because a <strong>ruptured ectopic pregnancy</strong> — a missed or irregular period, lower abdominal pain on one side, dark vaginal spotting, <strong>shoulder-tip pain</strong> from blood under the diaphragm, fainting on standing, a tender abdomen and the signs of concealed haemorrhage — kills within hours and looks, at first, like appendicitis or pelvic infection; the nurse sends the test, secures two cannulae and crossmatch, gives oxygen and fluid, calls the gynaecologist and prepares for theatre while the ultrasound is being arranged. <strong>Ovarian torsion</strong> and a <strong>ruptured ovarian cyst</strong> give sudden severe unilateral pain with vomiting, and <strong>pelvic inflammatory disease</strong> bilateral pain with fever, discharge and tenderness when the cervix is moved; a <strong>leaking abdominal aortic aneurysm</strong> gives collapse with abdominal or back pain and a pulsatile mass in an older hypertensive man and goes straight to theatre; and <strong>sickle cell crisis, lower-lobe pneumonia, diabetic ketoacidosis and malaria</strong> all imitate a surgical abdomen in Ghana and are excluded with a sickle test, a chest film, a glucose and a blood film before anyone operates. <strong>Upper gastrointestinal bleeding</strong> — <strong>haematemesis</strong> of fresh blood or coffee grounds, and <strong>melaena</strong>, the tarry, sticky, foul black stool of digested blood, which means the bleed has already happened even when nothing was vomited — comes from a <strong>peptic ulcer</strong> eroded by acid, <em>Helicobacter</em> and non-steroidals, or from <strong>oesophageal varices</strong> in the patient with a scarred liver (jaundice, ascites, spider naevi, a history of hepatitis B or alcohol), and the priorities are the same for both: the <strong>airway first</strong>, with a drowsy patient who is vomiting blood nursed on her side with suction at hand; <strong>two large cannulae</strong>, blood for haemoglobin, clotting and <strong>crossmatch</strong>, fluid now and blood as soon as it is available; <strong>nil by mouth</strong>; <strong>hourly</strong> pulse, blood pressure, conscious level and urine output, remembering that the pulse rises long before the pressure falls; a proton pump inhibitor for the ulcer and a vasoactive drug and antibiotics for the varices as prescribed; and early preparation for <strong>endoscopy</strong>, which is both the diagnosis and the treatment. Iron tablets also blacken the stool, but that stool is formed, not sticky, and the patient is not shocked; when in doubt it is a bleed.",
