            "<strong>Acute urinary retention</strong> is the commonest urological emergency and the most relieved patient in the department: the older man whose enlarged prostate finally closes after a night of beer, a cold tablet with an anticholinergic, or constipation, or the patient after a spinal anaesthetic, a hernia repair or a haemorrhoidectomy, with a tense, dull, tender swelling rising from the pelvis and the inability to pass urine or only an overflow dribble. The nurse offers privacy, warmth and analgesia, and <strong>catheterises aseptically</strong> with a well-lubricated 14 or 16 French catheter, never forcing it against resistance (a stricture or a false passage) — the doctor then places a <strong>suprapubic catheter</strong>; the bladder is drained fully and the residual volume recorded, the patient is watched for the <strong>post-obstructive diuresis</strong> of litres of dilute urine that follows a chronic retention, which needs fluid replacement, and for transient bleeding from the decompressed bladder wall; the cause is sought and treated, an alpha-blocker started, and a <strong>trial without catheter</strong> planned. <strong>Renal colic</strong> — a stone moving down the ureter — is the pain that makes a grown man roll on the floor, from loin to groin in waves, with vomiting and blood in the urine; the first treatment is <strong>analgesia</strong>, with a non-steroidal such as diclofenac by injection or suppository first and an opioid if needed, then fluids, straining of all urine for the stone, and imaging; most stones under 5 mm pass alone, but <strong>fever with renal colic</strong> means an infected kidney behind an obstruction and is a septic emergency needing antibiotics and a nephrostomy or stent within hours. <strong>Testicular torsion</strong> is a race: a boy or young man with sudden severe pain in one testis, nausea, a tender swollen testis that lies high and across, and no cremasteric reflex has <strong>about six hours</strong> before the testis dies, so he is kept nil by mouth, given analgesia and taken to theatre on clinical suspicion — an ultrasound that delays the operation costs the testis, and the examiner knows it. <strong>Paraphimosis</strong> is usually iatrogenic — a foreskin retracted for catheterisation and not replaced — and the nurse who always draws the foreskin forward after cleaning or catheterising prevents it; when it has happened, firm compression of the swollen glans in a swab, with ice or ordinary sugar to draw out the oedema, usually allows reduction, and a dorsal slit follows if it does not. <strong>Priapism</strong> beyond four hours, most often in sickle cell disease, is an emergency for the man's future sexual function and needs urgent aspiration. <strong>Fournier's gangrene</strong> — necrotising fasciitis of the scrotum and perineum in a diabetic man — spreads under skin that looks almost normal and kills by sepsis; it is an operation within hours. <strong>Clot retention</strong> after prostatic surgery is managed with bladder irrigation (lesson 6.3); and after a <strong>pelvic fracture</strong> or a fall astride, <strong>blood at the meatus</strong> means the urethra may be torn and no urethral catheter is passed.",
